From 622f62ddfcc27f04eed3cdee92321fdee74a1ea3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diego Amicabile <diego.amicabile@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 16:03:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Borg: explain why borg_check_rest() rates a monster at its
 own grid

Before resting, borg_check_rest() refuses when a monster within four
grids is too dangerous.  It asks borg_danger_one_kill() for the danger
at the monster's own grid, not at the resting grid.  That is deliberate:
at the resting grid a melee monster three or four grids away scores
almost nothing, while at its own grid it is scored as if adjacent, which
is the danger it poses once it closes in.

Replace the "one call for dangers" comment with one that says so.
Comment only; no code change.

Tracked as diegoami/neo-angband-tracker#486.
---
 src/borg/borg-flow-misc.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/borg/borg-flow-misc.c b/src/borg/borg-flow-misc.c
index bbc6e1eca..7951989d1 100644
--- a/src/borg/borg-flow-misc.c
+++ b/src/borg/borg-flow-misc.c
@@ -1331,7 +1331,9 @@ bool borg_check_rest(int y, int x)
         if (!kill->awake && d > 8 && !borg.munchkin_mode)
             continue;
 
-        /* one call for dangers */
+        /* Rate the monster at its own grid, not at (y, x): this is the
+         * danger it poses once it has closed in, so a melee monster a few
+         * grids away still counts against resting here. */
         p = borg_danger_one_kill(y9, x9, 1, i, true, true);
 
         /* Ignore proximity checks while inside a vault */
